 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "GROVE (behind the brambles). Private. By which we mean, the brambles are private. Bring secateurs."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 11,
   "local": {"cy": "Traphont Ddŵr Pontcysyllte"},
   "text": "PONTCYSYLLTE AQUEDUCT. 1805. Nineteen arches, 38 metres up. Boats cross in the sky and nobody bats an eyelid."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "TREFOR / TREVOR BASIN. Canal boats this way, tea rooms that way. Priorities."
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "WEST: Llangollen, 4 miles. DWYRAIN: Caer. Mind the geese."
  },
  {
   "x": 60,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "EAST: Chester. Welcome to Cymru, or goodbye, depending which way you're facing. Croeso / Hwyl."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "RIVER DEE (Afon Dyfrdwy). Say it 'DUV-ur-doo-ee'. Welsh spelling makes perfect sense once you know the rules. The river knows them."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "dee_valley_offas_dyke",
   "title": "Offa's Dyke",
   "text": "Offa, king of Mercia in the 700s, had a great bank and ditch dug along the Welsh border. Nobody is quite sure whether it was a defence, a trade line or showing off. The Offa's Dyke Path, a national trail of about 177 miles, crosses the Dee near here."
  }
 ],
